>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-muted font-body mb-4">
        <span class="bg-stone-100 text-wood-700 px-3 py-1">Winter</span>
        <span>November 2026</span>
        <span>&middot; 7 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="leading-tight mb-4">Winter in Central Portugal: what December, January and February are really like near Pombal</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted font-body">Short answer: <strong>mild and wet, not cold and definitely not beach weather</strong>. Around Pombal, December&ndash;February afternoons average <strong>14.6&ndash;15.9 &deg;C</strong>, nights drop to <strong>4.6&ndash;6.3 &deg;C</strong>, and each month brings <strong>105&ndash;127 mm of rain</strong>. In exchange you get monuments without queues, green hills, full rivers, a Christmas village inside medieval walls 76 minutes away &mdash; and the cheapest weeks of the year to stay.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-4 font-body">
      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Winter by the numbers</h2>
      <p>The nearest long climate series to Abiul is Coimbra, 60 km north &mdash; same Atlantic-facing basin, same altitude band. Its 1971&ndash;2000 normals (Instituto de Meteorologia) read like this:</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-stone-100 text-left">
              <th class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Month</th>
              <th class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Avg high</th>
              <th class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Avg low</th>
              <th class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Rain</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">November</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">18.0 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">7.7 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">109.4 mm</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">December</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">15.4 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">6.3 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">126.8 mm</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">January</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">14.6 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">4.6 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">112.2 mm</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">February</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">15.9 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">5.9 &deg;C</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">105.6 mm</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Two things matter more than the averages. First, <strong>the rain is concentrated</strong>: December is the wettest month of the year, and the Atlantic fronts tend to arrive in blocks of two or three wet days followed by clear, cold, dry spells. Second, <strong>daytime temperatures rarely collapse</strong>: 15 &deg;C in the afternoon in January is normal, which is why you can walk, cycle and eat outside on a sheltered terrace in a jumper. Clear nights in the valleys behind Abiul can bring ground frost, and the Serra de Sic&oacute; hills to the east run a degree or two colder than the coastal plain.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">What the landscape does</h2>
      <p>This is the greenest the region gets. The <a href="/garden/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">garden</a> keeps its structure and the grass grows back after the first rains; the olive harvest runs through December and January, with nets spread under the trees along every road out of the village; orange trees are loaded; and the rivers &mdash; the Arunca below the house, the Nab&atilde;o, the Alva &mdash; actually carry water again. Valley mist at dawn is the standard winter photograph here, and by 11:00 it has usually burned off.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Monuments without the queues</h2>
      <p>Between November and February, the UNESCO circuit around Pombal returns to local rhythm. Conímbriga, the largest Roman site in Portugal, is open <strong>10:00&ndash;18:00 with the ticket office closing at 17:15</strong>, and an adult ticket costs <strong>&euro;10</strong> (Museu Nacional de Conímbriga). It is <strong>37 km / 37 minutes</strong> from the house. In summer you share the mosaics with coach groups; in January you may have the House of the Fountains to yourself. Coimbra (60 km / 46 min), Alcoba&ccedil;a (83 km / 63 min), Batalha (61 km / 48 min) and Tomar (41 km / 49 min) behave the same way.</p>
      <p>One caveat worth planning around: public museums and monuments can close on 25 December and 1 January, and sometimes reduce hours during the Christmas week. Check the official site of whatever you plan to visit before driving &mdash; and use the <a href="/day-trips/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">day trips</a> section of this site, which lists drive times from the house, as your shortlist.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">The Christmas window: 27 November to 3 January</h2>
      <p>The single best winter date on the regional calendar is <strong>&Oacute;bidos Vila Natal</strong>, the Christmas village built inside the town&rsquo;s medieval walls. The 2026&ndash;27 edition runs <strong>27 November 2026 to 3 January 2027</strong> (theme: Pinocchio and the world of marionettes), with a Christmas market, a carousel, a grotto and a full programme of shows. &Oacute;bidos is <strong>109 km / 76 minutes</strong> from Abiul &mdash; an easy day out, and far better done from here than as a rushed stop on a Lisbon&ndash;Porto itinerary.</p>
      <p>F&aacute;tima (59 km / 45 min) is at its most atmospheric after dark in December, when the basilica plaza fills for the Christmas and New Year liturgies; Coimbra (60 km / 46 min) does lights, concerts and a Christmas market in the Baixa. If you want the week between Christmas and New Year here, book it early: it is the only stretch of the low season when rural houses around Pombal fill up, mostly with Portuguese families coming back from abroad.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Six winter day trips, with real drive times</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-stone-100 text-left">
              <th class="p-2 border border-stone-200">From Abiul</th>
              <th class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Distance</th>
              <th class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Why in winter</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Conímbriga</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">37 km / 37 min</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Mosaics and baths with no crowd, roofed museum for wet hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Coimbra</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">60 km / 46 min</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">University, old cathedral, bookshops, caf&eacute;s &mdash; the classic rainy-day city</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Tomar</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">41 km / 49 min</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Convent of Christ, empty cloisters, low winter light</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">F&aacute;tima</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">59 km / 45 min</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Quiet pilgrimage days, no coach traffic</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Bu&ccedil;aco / Luso</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">83 km / 62 min</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Walled forest, moss and mist &mdash; the <a href="/blog/bussaco-forest-luso-day-trip-pombal/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">best wet-weather walk</a> we know</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">&Oacute;bidos</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">109 km / 76 min</td><td class="p-2 border border-stone-200">Vila Natal, 27 Nov&ndash;3 Jan; walls and ginjinha the rest of the winter</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Distances and times are driving estimates from OpenStreetMap/OSRM, measured from the house, not from Pombal town.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">What winter closes &mdash; and what it opens</h2>
      <p>Be honest about the trade-off. The <strong>pool is a May&ndash;September affair</strong>, and we do not pretend otherwise: an unheated outdoor pool in January is a very short swim. The Atlantic beaches at Osso da Baleia and Pedr&oacute;&atilde;o (around an hour) are for walking, beachcombing and watching winter swells, not swimming &mdash; see the <a href="/beaches/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">beaches page</a> for which ones are worth the drive out of season.</p>
      <p>What winter opens is the interior: the <a href="/guide/pombal/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Pombal</a> and Sic&oacute; walking routes, the schist villages to the east, the river beaches as picnic stops rather than swimming spots, and restaurant tables without a booking. Prices follow the same logic: flights, car hire and accommodation are all at their annual low outside the Christmas week, which makes a week here in January cheaper than a long weekend in August.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">The house in winter</h2>
      <p>Casa dos Duques was built for this climate: <strong>thick stone walls</strong> that hold warmth, a <strong>fireplace in the living room</strong>, and a garden that is dormant rather than dead &mdash; citrus, olives, camellias. See the <a href="/the-house/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">house</a> and <a href="/amenities/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">amenities</a> pages for the practical list. In winter the village is a village again: the bakery queue is local, the roads are empty, and the loudest thing at night is rain on the roof.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Practical checklist</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Pack in layers</strong> &mdash; 15 &deg;C at 14:00 and 6 &deg;C at 22:00 on the same day is normal; a waterproof shell matters more than a heavy coat.</li>
        <li><strong>Plan drives for daylight</strong> &mdash; December sunsets come early, and country roads here have no lighting.</li>
        <li><strong>Alternate indoor and outdoor days</strong> &mdash; pair a wet morning at Conímbriga&rsquo;s museum with a dry afternoon walk, not the other way round.</li>
        <li><strong>Check official sites for holiday closures</strong> on 24&ndash;25 December and 1 January before setting out.</li>
        <li><strong>Book the Christmas&ndash;New Year week early</strong>; the rest of the season can be booked late, and is often discounted.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Winter is not the postcard version of Central Portugal &mdash; no sunbathing, no pool days. It is the working version: green, quiet, cheap, and with the monuments finally behaving like the places they were built to be. If you have been thinking about seeing this region without the summer crowd, <a href="/booking/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">check availability at Casa dos Duques</a> for December through February, and read the rest of the <a href="/guide/central-portugal/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Central Portugal guide</a> for the wider context.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 p-6 bg-stone-100">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted italic font-body">Sources: Coimbra climate normals 1971&ndash;2000, Instituto de Meteorologia (via pt.wikipedia.org &ldquo;Coimbra&rdquo;, consulted 16/11/2026); Museu Nacional de Con&iacute;mbriga, mnconimbriga.gov.pt/visitar (hours and &euro;10 adult ticket, consulted 16/11/2026); &Oacute;bidos Vila Natal 2026&ndash;27, vilanatal.obidos.pt (27/11/2026&ndash;03/01/2027). Distances and drive times: OpenStreetMap/OSRM, from Abiul. Pool season per our own amenities page (May&ndash;September). Weather figures are long-term averages, not a forecast &mdash; check IPMA before travelling.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

